<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duwa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1282">1282</a>–<a href="/years/1307">1307</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Duwa was a khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> in the late thirteenth and early fourteenth centuries. An ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaidu">Kaidu</a> in the wars against the Yuan, he also directed raids into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. His reign marked a period of renewed strength for the Chagatai Empire.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Duwa, also known as Du'a, was the second son of Baraq. In 1282 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaidu">Kaidu</a> appointed him head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>, hoping to make peace with the sons of Baraq, who had ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> for much of the previous ten years. The appointment kept the Chaghataids loyal to Kaidu until Kaidu's death. Duwa was already a seasoned commander: in 1275 he had destroyed a force in Uyghuria loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>, and with Kaidu he took Beshbalik from the Yuan the following year.</p><p>He then besieged Kara-Khoja near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> for six months with his brother Buzma. The Mongol princes failed to take the city by assault, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> ruler, Idiqut Khochqar, had the siege lifted only by giving Duwa his daughter in marriage, and probably by paying compensation as well.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In early 1295 Duwa invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaidu">Kaidu</a>'s son Sarban. He stayed eight months in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazandaran">Mazandaran</a> and pillaged many cities on the way back, sacking Fushang, before Kaidu recalled him. In the winter of 1298 he struck the Yuan garrisons, but was then defeated at what is now Kebuduo. In 1298 or 1299 he made his son Kutluk Khoja head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaraunas">Qara'unas</a>, a Mongol group that controlled much of Khurasan. He was wounded and defeated in a battle of 1301, shortly before Kaidu died.</p><p>Duwa passed over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaidu">Kaidu</a>'s chosen heir, Orus, and had Kaidu's firstborn son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chapar">Chapar</a> enthroned in 1303. Around 1304 a general peace among the Mongol states ended the Kaidu–Kublai war, which had lasted more than thirty years. Duwa then turned on Chapar: fighting broke out in 1305 or 1306, and with Yuan help he forced Chapar to surrender, deposing him in 1307 in favour of his brother Yangichar. Duwa died the same year and was succeeded by his son Könchek.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Duwa freed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> from its subservience to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaidu">Kaidu</a> and his sons, which had lasted since 1271, and restored relations with the Yuan for the first time since the mid-thirteenth century. His work proved transitory: less than forty years later the eastern part of the khanate split off, and in the 1360s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> reduced the western khans to puppets. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>'s memoirs trace the descent of his grandfather Yunas Khan from Duwa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Duwa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The combined forces of Kaidu's Ögedeid domains and the Chagatai khan <b>Duwa</b> (r. 1282–1307), whom Kaidu had installed, then waged sustained war against the Yuan dynasty of Kublai Khan and his successors in Central Asia, raiding the Uyghur lands and Mongolia and keeping pressure on the Yuan frontier…</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kaidu ruled from the Ili and Talas valleys, dominating the Chagatai Khanate through his ally <b>Duwa</b> and challenging Kublai Khan's claim to be Great Khan.</blockquote><a href="/state/ogedeid-khanate">Ogedeid Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
